<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yeongjo of Joseon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1694">1694</a>–<a href="/years/1776">1776</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as King Yeongjo</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yeongjo was the 21st king of Joseon <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>, ruling from 1724 to 1776, the longest reign of the dynasty. He pursued a policy of impartiality, tangpyeong, to curb factional strife at court, reduced the military cloth tax and promoted Confucian learning. In 1762 he had his son, Crown Prince Sado, sealed in a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> chest, where he died.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yeongjo-of-joseon.jpg" alt="Yeongjo of Joseon" width="320" height="570" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Chae Yong-sin / Jo Seokjin · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_King_Yeongjo_-_Chae_Yong_Shin_(%E8%94%A1%E9%BE%8D%E8%87%A3_1850-1941)_Cho_Seok-jin_(%E8%B6%99%E9%8C%AB%E6%99%89_1853-1920)_et_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
